var tafsirData_80 = {
  "1": "“He frowned and turned ˹his attention˺ away,” opens the surah with the gentle rebuke that the Quran directs at the Prophet, and the commentators establish the occasion with care. “He frowned and turned his attention away,” or “'abasa wa tawalla.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse refers to the incident of Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum, the blind man who came to the Prophet while he was speaking to the leaders of Quraysh, hoping to win them to Islam, and the Prophet frowned and turned away from the blind man to continue with the leaders, and the Quran gently corrected the Prophet for this, and the believer is taught that the surah is the correction of the messenger, and that the messenger who is corrected is the messenger who is taught, and that the correction is the mercy of the Lord to His prophet and to the community. Al-Tabari notes that the verses are addressed to the Prophet in the third person, and the believer is taught that the third person is the gentleness of the Lord, who corrected His servant without addressing him directly, and that the gentleness of the correction is the sign of the love.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the correction, for the verse teaches the believer that no one is above the correction of the truth, and that the Prophet himself, the best of creation, was corrected by his Lord, and that the believer is taught to receive the correction with the humility of the Prophet, and to know that the correction is the care of the Lord, and that the one who is not corrected is the one who is not loved. The verse also teaches the believer the danger of the preoccupation with the powerful, for the Prophet's turning was the turning of the one who hoped for the conversion of the leaders, and the believer is taught that the hope for the powerful must not come at the cost of the neglect of the sincere, and that the sincere seeker is the one who is not to be turned away. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to receive the correction of the truth with the humility, and to know that the one who frowns and turns is the one whom the Lord gently corrects, and that the believer is taught to guard his face and his attention against the turning that the verse describes.",
  "2": "“˹simply˺ because the blind man came to him ˹interrupting˺.” states the reason for the frown and the turning, and the verse names the man whose coming the Prophet had interrupted. “Simply because the blind man came to him interrupting,” or “an ja'ahul-a'ma.” Ibn Kathir explains that the blind man, Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum, came to the Prophet seeking to learn, and the believer is taught that the coming of the sincere seeker is the coming that should be welcomed, and that the blindness of the man was not a defect that lessened his right but a circumstance that increased the care due to him, and that the believer is taught to see the seeker and not the outward circumstance, and to know that the heart of the seeker is the sight that matters, and that the one who is blind in the eye may be the one who sees with the heart. Al-Tabari notes the word “al-a'ma” — “the blind” — and the believer is taught that the mention of the blindness is the mention of the excuse, for the blind man could not see the Prophet's preoccupation, and the believer is taught to make allowance for the one who cannot see the circumstance.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the seeker, for the verse teaches the believer that the sincere seeker is the most deserving of the attention, and that the one who comes to learn is the one who is not to be turned away for the sake of the one who may never come, and that the believer is taught to weigh the seeker by his sincerity and not by his standing, and to know that the blind seeker who came to learn was more precious to God than the leaders who were being courted. The verse also teaches the believer the honor of the one who seeks, for the whole surah was revealed because of the turning from the seeker, and the believer is taught that the seeker of the truth is honored by the Lord, and that the one who honors the seeker honors the Lord of the seeking. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to welcome the seeker, and to know that the one who comes to learn is the one whom the truth is for, and that the believer is taught to give his attention to the sincere and not to sell the sincere for the hope of the powerful.",
  "3": "“You never know ˹O Prophet˺, perhaps he may be purified,” states the first of the possibilities that the Prophet could not know, and the verse opens the heart of the correction. “You never know O Prophet, perhaps he may be purified,” or “wa ma yudreeka la'allahu yazzakka.” Ibn Kathir explains that the Prophet could not know that the blind man might be purified by what he would learn, and the believer is taught that the hidden outcomes of the hearts are known only to God, and that the messenger is not to judge the worth of the seeker by the outward, and that the one who is turned away may be the one who would have been purified, and that the believer is taught to leave the outcomes to the Lord and to give the attention to every seeker. Al-Tabari notes the word “yazzakka” — “he purifies himself” — and the believer is taught that the purification is the goal of the learning, and that the seeker who comes to learn is the seeker of the purification, and that the believer is taught to help the seeker to the purification and not to turn him from it.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the unknown, for the verse teaches the believer the limit of the human knowledge, and that the worth of a soul is hidden, and that the believer is taught to treat every soul as the one who may be purified, and to know that the one whom he turns away may be the one whom God would have purified through him, and that the turning away is the loss of the opportunity that the believer cannot measure. The verse also teaches the believer the hope that the messenger is taught to hold, for the seeker may be purified, and the believer is taught to hope for the purification of every seeker, and to know that the hope is the motive of the attention, and that the one who hopes is the one who attends. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to hope for the purification of the seeker, and to know that the unknown is the field of the hope, and that the believer is taught to give the attention and to leave the outcome, and that the attention is the duty and the purification is the gift.",
  "4": "“or he may be mindful, benefitting from the reminder.” states the second of the possibilities, and the verse adds the mindfulness to the purification. “Or he may be mindful, benefitting from the reminder,” or “aw yaththakkaru fa-tanfa'ahuth-thikra.” Ibn Kathir explains that the blind man might have been reminded and benefited from the reminder, and the believer is taught that the reminder of God is the benefit of the seeker, and that the one who is reminded is the one who is benefited, and that the believer is taught to give the reminder to every seeker, and to know that the reminder which is given to the sincere is the reminder that bears its fruit. Al-Tabari notes the word “yaththakkaru” — “he remembers,” “he takes heed” — and the believer is taught that the remembrance is the purpose of the message, and that the seeker who is reminded is the seeker who is saved, and that the believer is taught to be the means of the remembrance and not the barrier to it.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the benefit, for the verse teaches the believer the value of the reminder, and that the reminder which is given to the sincere is the reminder which benefits, and that the believer is taught to measure his words by the benefit, and to know that the word which is given to the powerful and the heedless may be the word that is lost, while the word which is given to the humble and the seeking is the word that is kept, and that the believer is taught to give the word where it is kept. The verse also teaches the believer the two gifts of the seeking, for the seeker may be purified and may be reminded, and the believer is taught that the purification and the remembrance are the two gifts that the seeker seeks, and that the believer is taught to give the attention that opens both, and to know that the one who gives the attention to the seeker is the one who is the means of the two gifts. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to give the reminder to the seeker, and to know that the reminder is the benefit and the benefit is the goal, and that the believer is taught to be of those who benefit the seekers and are benefited by the Lord.",
  "5": "“As for the one who was indifferent,” turns from the seeker to the one who was preferred, and the verse names the indifferent. “As for the one who was indifferent,” or “amma mani istaghna.” Ibn Kathir explains that the one who was indifferent is the wealthy leader who thought himself self-sufficient and in no need of the message, and the believer is taught that the self-sufficiency is the disease of the heedless, and that the one who thinks himself rich is the one who is poor, and that the believer is taught to see the poverty beneath the wealth, and to know that the one who is indifferent to the truth is the one who has declared his own sufficiency and his own ruin. Al-Tabari notes the word “istaghna” — “he considered himself self-sufficient” — and the believer is taught that the self-sufficiency is the claim of the heart that it has no need of God, and that the claim is the beginning of the loss.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the indifferent, for the verse teaches the believer the difference between the seeker and the indifferent, and that the seeker is the one who knows his need and the indifferent is the one who denies it, and that the believer is taught to know his need of God and to seek, and to flee the self-sufficiency that the verse names, and to know that the one who is indifferent to the truth is the one from whom the truth is indifferent. The verse also teaches the believer the danger of the wealth, for the wealth is the mother of the self-sufficiency, and the believer is taught to see his wealth as the gift and not the self, and to know that the one who is made rich by the world is the one who is made poor by the world, and that the believer is taught to be rich in the need of God and poor in the self-sufficiency. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to flee the indifference, and to know that the one who is indifferent to the reminder is the one whom the reminder passes, and that the believer is taught to seek and to need, and to know that the need is the door of the guidance.",
  "6": "“you gave him your ˹undivided˺ attention,” records the attention that the Prophet had given to the indifferent, and the verse completes the contrast that the surah is drawing. “You gave him your undivided attention,” or “fa-anta lahu tasadda.” Ibn Kathir explains that the Prophet had turned to the indifferent leader and given him his attention, and the believer is taught that the attention which is given to the indifferent is the attention which is spent on the one who does not want it, and that the believer is taught to direct his attention to the seeker and not to waste it on the indifferent, and to know that the attention is a trust to be placed where it bears fruit. Al-Tabari notes the word “tasadda” — “you turn to him,” “you attend to him” — and the believer is taught that the turning to the indifferent was the turning that the surah corrects, and that the correction is the lesson, and that the believer is taught to turn to the seeker and to let the indifferent go.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the attention, for the verse teaches the believer the stewardship of the attention, and that the attention is among the most precious of the believer's possessions, and that the believer is taught to spend it on the one who seeks and not on the one who scorns, and to know that the attention which is given to the indifferent is the attention which is lost, while the attention which is given to the seeker is the attention which is stored. The verse also teaches the believer the measure of the call, for the messenger is not to chase the one who flees and neglect the one who comes, and the believer is taught to call the one who is near and to leave the one who is far, and to know that the wisdom of the call is the wisdom that attends to the seeking heart. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to direct his attention to the seeker, and to know that the one who is indifferent to the truth is the one whom the truth may pass, and that the believer is taught to attend to the one who attends to the truth.",
  "7": "“even though you are not to blame if he would not be purified.” lightens the weight of the correction, and the verse reminds the Prophet that the outcome was not his burden. “Even though you are not to blame if he would not be purified,” or “wa ma 'alayka alla yazzakka.” Ibn Kathir explains that the Prophet was not responsible for the purification of the indifferent, and the believer is taught that the messenger's duty is the delivery and not the purification, and that the one who refuses the purification is the one who bears his own refusal, and that the believer is taught to deliver and to leave the outcome, and to know that the burden of the refusal is not on the caller. Al-Tabari notes the word “'alayka” — “upon you” — and the believer is taught that the messenger is not charged with the guidance of the hearts, and that the guidance is in the hand of God, and that the believer is taught to do his duty and to rest from the outcome.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the burden, for the verse teaches the believer the freedom of the caller, and that the caller who has delivered the message has done his duty, and that the response is not his work, and that the believer is taught to deliver with the sincerity and to leave the result, and to know that the one who delivers and rests is the one who is at peace, while the one who delivers and carries the outcome is the one who is crushed. The verse also teaches the believer the mercy of the correction, for the correction of the Prophet is softened by the reminder that the outcome was not his burden, and the believer is taught that the correction of God is always with the mercy, and that the believer is taught to correct with the mercy and to remind of the limits. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to deliver and to rest, and to know that the purification is the gift of God and not the work of the caller, and that the believer is taught to call and to trust.",
  "8": "“But as for the one who came to you, eager ˹to learn˺,” returns to the seeker, and the verse completes the contrast with the one who came. “But as for the one who came to you, eager to learn,” or “wa amma man ja'aka yas'a.” Ibn Kathir explains that the one who came was the one who strove in his coming, and the believer is taught that the coming of the seeker is the striving of the one who wants the truth, and that the believer is taught to honor the striving, and to know that the one who strives to reach the truth is the one whom the truth reaches, and that the believer is taught to welcome the striver and not to turn him away. Al-Tabari notes the word “yas'a” — “he strives,” “he hurries” — and the believer is taught that the seeking of the truth is the striving of the one who has known the value of what he seeks, and that the believer is taught to value the striver and to help him.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the coming, for the verse teaches the believer the difference between the one who comes and the one who turns, and that the one who comes with the striving is the one who deserves the attention, and that the believer is taught to measure the people by their seeking, and to know that the seeker is the treasure that the caller is given, and that the believer is taught to treat the seeker as the treasure. The verse also teaches the believer the honor of the seeking, for the surah has contrasted the striver with the indifferent, and the believer is taught that the striving is the mark of the one whom God loves, and that the believer is taught to be of the strivers, and to know that the striving to the truth is the striving that God sees and rewards. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to welcome the seeker and to honor the striving, and to know that the one who comes to the truth is the one whom the truth is for, and that the believer is taught to be the means of the seeker's reaching.",
  "9": "“being in awe ˹of Allah˺,” describes the state of the seeker, and the verse names the fear that accompanied his coming. “Being in awe of Allah,” or “wa huwa yakhsha.” Ibn Kathir explains that the blind man came in the fear of God, and the believer is taught that the fear of God is the mark of the seeker, and that the one who seeks the truth with the fear is the one who is prepared for the truth, and that the believer is taught to seek with the fear, and to know that the fear is the beginning of the wisdom, and that the one who fears is the one whom the truth finds ready. Al-Tabari notes the word “yakhsha” — “he fears,” “he stands in awe” — and the believer is taught that the fear of the seeker is the humility of the heart, and that the humility is the condition of the receiving, and that the believer is taught to humble his heart before the truth.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the fear, for the verse teaches the believer the inner state of the seeker, and that the seeking which is accompanied by the fear is the seeking which reaches, and that the believer is taught to join the fear to the seeking, and to know that the one who seeks without the fear seeks with the pride, and that the pride closes the door that the fear opens. The verse also teaches the believer the value of the fear, for the surah has described the seeker by his fear, and the believer is taught that the fear of God is the title of the one whom God honors, and that the believer is taught to cultivate the fear, and to know that the fear is the guard of the heart and the key of the receiving. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to seek with the fear, and to know that the one who comes to the truth in the fear is the one whom the truth welcomes, and that the believer is taught to be of those who fear and seek.",
  "10": "“you were inattentive to him.” closes the contrast with the correction, and the verse states what the Prophet had done to the seeker. “You were inattentive to him,” or “fa-anta 'anhu talahha.” Ibn Kathir explains that the Prophet had been distracted from the blind man by the leaders, and the believer is taught that the distraction from the seeker is the fault that the surah corrects, and that the believer is taught to guard against the distraction, and to know that the one who is distracted from the seeker by the powerful has sold the near for the far, and that the believer is taught to keep his attention on the one who seeks and not to let the hope of the powerful steal it. Al-Tabari notes the word “talahha” — “you turn away,” “you are distracted” — and the believer is taught that the distraction is the turning of the attention to the other, and that the turning is the lesson that the surah has been teaching.\n\nAl-Razi observes the completion of the lesson, for the surah has laid the two paths before the believer — the attention to the indifferent and the inattention to the seeker — and the believer is taught to reverse them, to give the attention to the seeker and to let the indifferent go, and to know that the reversal is the lesson of the surah, and that the one who learns the lesson has learned the manner of the call, and that the believer is taught to put the seeking heart above the powerful head. The verse also teaches the believer the mercy of the Lord, for the correction of the Prophet was the correction that raised the seeker, and the believer is taught that the Lord honors the seeker and corrects even His prophet for the sake of the seeker, and that the believer is taught to honor the seeker as the Lord honors him. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to give his attention to the seeker, and to know that the one who comes to the truth is the one whom the truth is for, and that the believer is taught to be the means of the seeker's good, and to never be distracted from the seeking heart by the hope of the powerful.",
  "11": "\"But no! This ˹revelation˺ is truly a reminder.\" declares the nature of the revelation after correcting the frown, clarifying that the revelation, not the indifferent, is the focus. The statement is emphatic.\n\n“But no! This revelation is truly a reminder” translates “kalla innaha tadhkirah,” No! Indeed it is a reminder, as with “Enough! Surely this Quran is a reminder” and “Surely this is a reminder. So let whoever wills take the Right Way,” and the Ark as reminder for attentive ears, and the Pen and what everyone writes, and the Reminder for the whole world. Revelation here is the same Reminder that would humble a mountain, the weighty revelation soon sent, the Light that brings from darkness, and the Balance.\n\nBut no, or “kalla,” denies the prioritization that the indifferent is more worthy than the eager in awe, and denies that the revelation is ancient fables because blessed with wealth, or pure magic, or word of a man.\n\nTruly a reminder indicates the revelation’s function is to remind, like the garden reduced to ashes while they slept as reminder, the stars put out, sky torn, mountains blown, and the messengers’ time.\n\nThis follows you were inattentive to him to show that the revelation, not the status of the listener, is the reminder, and precedes “So let whoever wills be mindful of it,” inviting response.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear reminder as information only, like the personal letters wished for. Reminder is the mighty catastrophe warning to humankind, to whichever chooses to take the lead or lag, and the Day every soul will be detained.\n\nFor the living who are told to make room, give rightful share, and walk upright, reminder should make you mindful, as with the self-reproaching soul and the in-awe without seeing, so reminder becomes your Gardens held in honour where reminder is “All this is surely a reward, your striving appreciated,” not the woe on that Day to deniers who turned away from the reminder as spooked zebras.",
  "12": "\"So let whoever wills be mindful of it.\" invites response after declaring the revelation a reminder. The will is yours.\n\n“So let whoever wills be mindful of it” translates “fa-man sha’a dhakarah,” whoever wills may remember it, be mindful, like “Surely this is a reminder. So let whoever wills take the Right Way,” and “let whoever wills take the path leading back to their Lord,” the Day ultimate truth. Will, or “sha’a,” is choice, as with every soul detained for what it has done and the Day every person will remember striving.\n\nWhoever wills indicates the reminder respects volition, as with the test of best deeds, the choice between grateful and ungrateful, and the Day fifty thousand years.\n\nBe mindful, or “dhakara,” is to remember, to be humbled at remembrance, to listen and reason so you are not among residents of Blaze who lament if only we had listened, and to be among those in awe without seeing with mighty reward.\n\nThis follows This revelation is truly a reminder to show what is to be mindful of.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think mindful is automatic, like personal letters spreading scriptures for all to read. Mindful requires will, as with the right Way while you will.\n\nFor practice, will to be mindful now. Remember the Name morning and evening, prostrate part of night, glorify long, give rightful share, and walk upright, so willing becomes the lead that is Gardens held in honour, not the lag that is detained, and the reminder that is enough becomes your light perfected, where the blind man who may be purified and may be mindful benefitting from the reminder is your model, not the indifferent who was given undue attention.\n\nAdditional: So let whoever wills be mindful also parallels “Surely this is a reminder. So let whoever wills take the Right Way” and “let whoever wills take the path leading back to their Lord,” that Day ultimate truth, and the Garden owners’ hope for a better garden after turning. Willing is choice, as with every soul detained for what it has done, and you will be presented with no secret hidden, and the Day every person will remember striving. Willing here is the Right Guidance that leads to Gardens, vineyards, maidens, full cups, never hearing idle talk, a generous gift, where willing becomes the Gardens held in honour and the vast kingdom and bliss, fine silk, silver bracelets, and pure drink, where reminder that would humble a mountain becomes light perfected.",
  "13": "\"It is ˹written˺ on pages held in honour—\" describes where the reminder is preserved after inviting whoever wills to be mindful. The pages are honoured.\n\n“It is written on pages held in honour—” translates “fi suhufin mukarramah,” it is in honored scrolls, as with the well-preserved Record only purified touch, the Pen and what everyone writes, and the Spread scriptures wished for as personal letters. Pages held in honour, or “suhuf mukarramah,” are the heavenly scrolls, honoured, like the Gardens held in honour for the excepted who pray, and the vast kingdom and bliss, fine silk, silver bracelets, and pure drink. Held in honour indicates they are exalted, not despised like ancient fables, and are the opposite of the despicable oath-taker, slanderer, withholder.\n\nWritten indicates the Pen has written, as with the Reminder for the whole world.\n\nPages held in honour follow let whoever wills be mindful to show where mindfulness leads.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to confuse pages held in honour with personal letters spread scriptures for all to read wished for. Pages held in honour are the well-preserved Record, not personal letters to each denier who will not believe.\n\nFor the believer who will take the Right Way while you will, pages held in honour should inspire awe, as with the mountain that would be humbled, the sky built seven mighty, and the Pen’s oath. Treat the earthly pages that carry the reminder—your copy, your recitation, your night standing properly—as honoured, reciting properly, remembering wholeheartedly, taking Trustee, so pages held in honour become your Gardens held in honour where your striving has been appreciated and you are told “All this is surely a reward for you.”\n\nMore: Pages held in honour also parallels the Gardens held in honour for the excepted who pray, and the vast kingdom and bliss, fine silk, silver bracelets, and pure drink. Held in honour, or “mukarramah,” is exalted, not despised like ancient fables, and are the opposite of the despicable oath-taker, slanderer, withholder. Held in honour indicates they are exalted, like the Gardens held in honour, the elevated canopy where fruit hangs within reach. For the believer who will take the Right Way while you will, pages held in honour should inspire awe, as with the mountain that would be humbled, the sky built seven mighty, and the Pen’s oath, so pages held in honour become your Gardens held in honour where striving appreciated is yours.",
  "14": "\"highly esteemed, purified—\" further qualifies the pages held in honour after “It is written on pages held in honour.” The esteem is exaltation and purity.\n\n“highly esteemed, purified—” translates “marfu‘atin mutahharah,” exalted, elevated, and purified, as with the well-preserved Record only purified touch, the Pen, and the reminder. Highly esteemed, or “marfu‘ah,” is raised high, like the sky raised high and formed flawlessly, the seven mighty heavens, the Throne borne by eight, and the Gardens’ elevated canopy where fruit hangs within reach. Purified, or “mutahharah,” is cleansed, as with purify your garments commanded to the covered, the well-preserved Record only purified angels touch, and the pure drink from the Lord.\n\nEsteemed and purified indicates the pages are not the choking food, the raging Flame, the dunes, or the despicable’s ancient fables, but high and clean, like the Garden’s silk and silver bracelets, and the eternal youths as scattered pearls.\n\nThis follows pages held in honour to add two attributes, and precedes by the hands of angel-scribes, honourable and virtuous, showing who handles them.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think highly esteemed means locked away, not for the mindful. Highly esteemed means they are worthy to be sought by the eager in awe who came, the blind man who may be purified and mindful, not the indifferent who was given undue attention.\n\nFor practice, esteem and purify your own pages—your heart, your copy, your recitation—by being among the grateful who fulfil vows, fear sweeping horror, and give food despite desire, so esteemed, purified pages become your vast kingdom and bliss, where the reminder you were mindful of becomes your reward, and the Pen writes your mindfulness as honour.\n\nFurther: Highly esteemed, purified also qualifies the pages held in honour, highly esteemed, purified, as with the well-preserved Record only purified touch, the Pen, and the reminder. Highly esteemed, or “marfu‘ah,” is raised high, like the sky raised high and formed flawlessly, the seven mighty heavens, the Throne borne by eight, and the Gardens’ elevated canopy where fruit hangs within reach. Purified, or “mutahharah,” is cleansed, as with purify your garments commanded to the covered, the well-preserved Record. For the mindful, esteemed and purified pages become your vast kingdom and bliss, where the reminder you were mindful of becomes your reward, and the Pen writes your mindfulness as honour, and the difficult Day far from easy becomes easy, and the raging Flame that rips scalps is not yours.",
  "15": "\"by the hands of angel-scribes,\" identifies who handles the highly esteemed, purified pages held in honour. The scribes are angels.\n\n“by the hands of angel-scribes” translates “bi-aydi safarah,” by hands of ambassadors, scribes, as with the pen and what everyone writes and what purified angels touch, the well-preserved Record, and the noble Messenger’s recitation. Angel-scribes, or “safarah,” are intermediaries who write, as with the angels stripping harshly and pulling gently, gliding, leading, conducting, the stern guards and shooting stars, the formidable severe angels over the Fire, and the eight bearing the Throne, the holy spirit and angels standing in ranks.\n\nBy the hands indicates the pages are handled with honour, not by the despicable withholder, slanderer, gossip-monger, or the garden owners whispering to exclude poor. Angel-scribes contrasts with the despicable oath-taker and the brute.\n\nThis follows highly esteemed, purified to show who is worthy to touch them, and precedes honourable and virtuous, describing them.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to confuse angel-scribes with human scribes who copy the Record in the unseen to justify “Do you have a scripture in which you read whatever you choose?” Angel-scribes are purified, well-preserved, while human copying of unseen is false.\n\nFor the mindful who will take the Right Way, the hands of angel-scribes should inspire trust, as with the weighty revelation soon sent, the Reminder for the whole world, and the Pen’s oath, that the reminder you were inattentive to the blind for is actually carried by honoured, pure hands, so cherish it, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so your hands, like theirs, become virtuous, and your Gardens are held in honour where hands are adorned with silver bracelets and your Lord gives you a pure drink.\n\nMore: Angel-scribes, or “safarah,” also are intermediaries who write, as with the angels stripping harshly and pulling gently, gliding, leading, conducting, the stern guards and shooting stars, the formidable severe angels over the Fire, and the eight bearing the Throne, the holy spirit and angels standing in ranks. Angel-scribes are the handlers of the honoured pages, as with the pen and what everyone writes and what purified angels touch, the well-preserved Record, and the noble Messenger’s recitation. Their hands indicate honour, not by the despicable withholder. For the mindful who will take the Right Way, the hands of angel-scribes should inspire trust, as with the weighty revelation soon sent, the Reminder for the whole world, and the Pen’s oath, that the reminder you were inattentive to the blind for is carried by honoured, pure hands.",
  "16": "\"honourable and virtuous.\" describes the angel-scribes after “by the hands of angel-scribes.” The two traits define them.\n\n“honourable and virtuous” translates “kiramin bararah,” noble, honourable and dutiful, righteous, as with the noble Messenger not insane, of outstanding character, never-ending reward, the sincere devout like Mary who guarded chastity and testified, the wife of Pharaoh’s house near Allah, and the helpers who loved emigrants. Honourable, or “kiramin,” is noble, esteemed, as with pages held in honour, highly esteemed, the Gardens held in honour, the vast kingdom and bliss, fine silk, silver bracelets. Virtuous, or “bararah,” is dutiful, obedient, as with the virtuous who drink camphor, the grateful, the servants who drink at will, the fulfilled vows, sweeping horror feared, and the excepted who pray.\n\nThis follows angel-scribes to show why they are worthy to handle the esteemed, purified pages, and precedes “Condemned are disbelieving humans! How ungrateful they are,” contrasting honourable, virtuous angels with condemned, ungrateful humans.\n\nHonourable and virtuous indicates that the Pages’ handling reflects handling, so the Reminder is honourable and virtuous, not despicable oath-taker, slanderer, nor ancient fables.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think honourable and virtuous are exclusive to angels, not a model. The Quran calls Mary one of the devout, the helpers virtuous, and the Gardensexcellent provision for the devout, so virtue is for you.\n\nFor the eager in awe who came to be purified and mindful, honourable and virtuous are your aspiration. Be honourable and virtuous, fulfil vows, give food despite desire, protect families, give rightful share, walk upright, so your hands, though human, mimic the angel-scribes’ hands, and your pages, though earthly copies, reflect the honoured, esteemed, purified pages, and your vast kingdom and bliss become your honourable, virtuous reward, where the reminder you were mindful of becomes your Gardens held in honour.\n\nAdditional: Honourable and virtuous also describes the angel-scribes, noble, honourable and dutiful, righteous, as with the noble Messenger not insane, of outstanding character, never-ending reward, the sincere devout like Mary who guarded chastity and testified, the wife of Pharaoh’s house near Allah, and the helpers who loved emigrants. Honourable, or “kiramin,” is noble, esteemed, as with pages held in honour, highly esteemed, the Gardens held in honour, the vast kingdom. Virtuous, or “bararah,” is dutiful, obedient, as with the virtuous who drink camphor, the grateful, the servants who drink at will, the fulfilled vows. For the eager in awe who came to be purified and mindful, honourable and virtuous are your aspiration, so your hands, though human, mimic the angel-scribes’ hands, and your Gardens are held in honour where your striving has been appreciated.",
  "17": "\"Condemned are ˹disbelieving˺ humans! How ungrateful they are ˹to Allah˺!\" is a cry against ingratitude after describing honourable, virtuous angel-scribes handling esteemed pages. The contrast is stark.\n\n“Condemned are disbelieving humans! How ungrateful they are to Allah!” translates “qutila al-insanu ma akfarah,” cursed, killed, is man, how ungrateful, how disbelieving he is. Condemned, or “qutila,” is like “May he be condemned! How evil was what he determined!” for the degrader, and “Woe to you, and more woe!” Ungrateful, or “akfarah,” is intense ingratitude, covering the Way shown grateful or ungrateful, where ungrateful chooses chains, shackles, blazing Fire, while grateful chooses camphor, Salsabil, radiance, Garden and silk.\n\nThis follows honourable and virtuous to highlight that humans who are disbelieving are opposite to honourable, virtuous angels, and precedes “From what substance did He create them? He created them from a sperm-drop, and ordained their development,” which humbles the condemned by origin, similar to “Is there not a period when each human is nothing worth mentioning?” and “Did We not create you from a humble fluid placed in secure place.”\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear condemned as hopeless, like the luxury deniers left for a little while with shackles. Condemned here is a rebuke that can be answered with “Let whoever wills be mindful,” recite what is easy, and take the Right Way, as with the blind man who may be purified.\n\nFor the one created from humble fluid, condemned ungrateful should make you grateful, fulfilling vows, fearing sweeping horror, giving food despite desire, so condemned becomes delivered, radiance, joy, Garden and silk, where ungrateful is replaced by the virtuous’ drink and the striving appreciated, where the Pages held in honour become your pages remembered, not your condemnation.\n\nFurther: Condemned are disbelieving humans also contrasts with the honourable, virtuous angels, showing that humans who are disbelieving are opposite to honourable, virtuous angels, and precedes “From what substance did He create them? He created them from a sperm-drop, and ordained their development,” which humbles the condemned by origin. Condemned, or “qutila,” is like “May he be condemned! How evil was what he determined!” for the degrader, and “Woe to you, and more woe!” Ungrateful, or “akfarah,” is intense ingratitude, covering the Way shown grateful or ungrateful, where ungrateful chooses chains, shackles, blazing Fire, while grateful chooses camphor, Salsabil. For the living, condemned ungrateful should make you grateful, fulfilling vows, giving food despite desire, so condemned becomes delivered, radiance, Garden and silk, where ungrateful is replaced by the virtuous’ drink and the striving appreciated.",
  "18": "\"From what substance did He create them?\" asks after condemning ungrateful humans, prompting reflection on origin.\n\n“From what substance did He create them?” translates “min ayyi shay’in khalaqah,” from what thing did He create him, as with “Is there not a period when each human is nothing worth mentioning?” and “Did We not create you from a humble fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, How excellent are We in doing so,” and “Were they not once a sperm-drop emitted? Then a clinging clot, then He developed and perfected, producing both sexes,” and “Producing from it both sexes” and “Is such a Creator unable to bring the dead back to life?”\n\nFrom what substance indicates the humble fluid, the sperm-drop, the mixed fluids to test, so made hear and see, shown the Way grateful or ungrateful.\n\nThis follows condemned ungrateful to humble, and precedes “He created them from a sperm-drop, and ordained their development,” giving the answer.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as insult without purpose. It humbles the one who was indifferent, given undivided attention though not to blame if not purified, the despicable withholder, and the arrogant, to make room for the eager in awe.\n\nFor the indifferent who thought honour expels inferior and merchandise better than what is with Allah, from what substance reminds you that you came from humble fluid in a secure place, so the Garden’s vast kingdom and bliss, not the raging Flame that rips scalps, should be sought by being among the grateful who give rightful share, protect families, and walk upright, so humble origin becomes the honoured pages’ esteemed, purified pages, not condemnation.\n\nMore: From what substance did He create them also echoes “Is there not a period when each human is nothing worth mentioning?” and “Did We not create you from a humble fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, How excellent are We in doing so,” and “Were they not once a sperm-drop emitted? Then a clinging clot, then He developed and perfected, producing both sexes,” and “Producing from it both sexes” and “Is such a Creator unable to bring the dead back to life?” From what substance indicates the humble fluid, the sperm-drop, the mixed fluids to test, so made hear and see, shown the Way grateful or ungrateful. For the indifferent who thought honour expels inferior and merchandise better than what is with Allah, from what substance reminds you that you came from humble fluid in a secure place, so the Garden’s vast kingdom and bliss, not the raging Flame, should be sought by being among the grateful who give rightful share, protect families, and walk upright.",
  "19": "\"He created them from a sperm-drop, and ordained their development.\" answers “From what substance did He create them?” The answer is drop and ordainment.\n\n“He created them from a sperm-drop, and ordained their development” translates “min nutfatin khalaqahu fa-qaddarah,” from a drop He created him and determined, proportioned him, as with the mixed fluids to test, hearing and seeing, shown the Way, the humble fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, How excellent are We, the sperm-drop emitted, clinging clot, developed and perfected, producing both sexes, and the Pen. Ordained, or “qaddara,” is to measure, to proportion, as with the Pen and what everyone writes, the well-preserved Record, the appointed time that never delays, and the crystalline silver filled precisely as desired.\n\nSperm-drop and ordained development show the Most Capable who holds all authority, Most Capable of restoring fingertips, who created death and life to test, who adorned lowest heaven with lamps, who holds birds, and who is Lord of pathways.\n\nThis follows from what substance to give the answer.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read sperm-drop as low, leading to despair, like the garden reduced to ashes. Sperm-drop is the humble beginning that the Most Compassionate honours by ordaining with excellence, as with the period when each human is nothing worth mentioning then hearing and seeing.\n\nFor the condemned ungrateful who love fleeting and neglect weighty Day, this answer should make you grateful, fulfilling vows, giving food despite desire, so sperm-drop and ordained development become your Gardens held in honour where ordained is Garden and silk, reclining, shade, fruit, silver, crystal, ginger, Salsabil, eternal youths, bliss and kingdom, fine silk, silver bracelets, pure drink, where the drop’s ordaining is remembered as the striving appreciated, “All this is surely a reward, your striving appreciated.”\n\nExtra: He created them from a sperm-drop, and ordained their development also answers “From what substance did He create them?” The answer is drop and ordainment, from a drop He created him and determined, proportioned him, as with the mixed fluids to test, hearing and seeing, shown the Way, the humble fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, How excellent are We, the sperm-drop emitted, clinging clot, developed and perfected, producing both sexes, and the Pen. Ordained, or “qaddara,” is to measure, to proportion, as with the Pen and what everyone writes, the well-preserved Record, the appointed time that never delays, and the crystalline silver filled precisely as desired. For the condemned ungrateful who love fleeting and neglect weighty Day, this answer should make you grateful, fulfilling vows, giving food despite desire, so sperm-drop and ordained development become your Gardens held in honour where ordained is Garden and silk, reclining, shade, fruit, silver, crystal, ginger, Salsabil, eternal youths, bliss and kingdom, where the drop’s ordaining is remembered as the striving appreciated.",
  "20": "\"Then He makes the way easy for them,\" continues the human story after sperm-drop and ordained development. The way made easy is the test’s path.\n\n“Then He makes the way easy for them” translates “thumma al-sabila yassarah,” then the Way He eased for him, as with “We already showed them the Way, whether they choose to be grateful or ungrateful,” the Straight Path walked upright, not crawling facedown, the Balance so people may administer justice, the Light, the Reminder, the Pen, the weighty revelation, and the spaced earth for spacious pathways to walk along, brought forth water and pastures, mountains set firmly, all as sustenance for you and your animals.\n\nMakes the way easy, or “yassarah,” is to make smooth, to ease, as with after hardship He will bring about ease, He will make their matters easy for the mindful, and the Garden’s fruit made very easy to reach.\n\nThis follows sperm-drop and ordained development to show that after forming, the Way is eased, not that honour expels inferior or merchandise is better.\n\nThen He makes the way easy indicates that the One who perfectly ordained humble fluid also eases the Way, so the test is fair, as with the appointed time until which humble fluid stays.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to take easy way as meaning no shackles, raging Fire, dunes. Way easy is for the grateful, while the ungrateful face the difficult Day far from easy.\n\nFor the one created from humble fluid, the way made easy should be taken, as with “let whoever wills take the Right Way to your Lord” and “let whoever wills be mindful of it” on pages held in honour by angel-scribes honourable and virtuous. Take the easy Way now, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so easy becomes the Gardens held in honour where easy fruit and easy shade remain, not the unbearable fate and Saqar that does not let live.",
  "21": "\"then causes them to die and be buried.\" continues the human journey after the way made easy. The stage is death and burial.\n\n“then causes them to die and be buried” translates “thumma amatahu fa-aqbarah,” then He makes him die and buries him, as with “We have ordained death for all of you and We cannot be prevented,” the soul reaches the throat while you are looking on, nearer than you but you cannot see, bring that soul back if you claim not subject, and the dying person realizes it is time to depart, feet tied, driven to your Lord. Causes to die and be buried indicates that the One who created from a sperm-drop and ordained, then eased the Way, then causes death as part of creation of death and life to test which is best in deeds, and as with the earth a lodging for the living and the dead, the earth smoothed like a bed, and the flood that overflowed where We carried you in the Ark.\n\nBe buried, or “aqbarah,” is to be given a grave, a lodging in the earth that was lodging for the living and the dead, the secure place’s final return, as with “He will return you to it, and then simply bring you forth again.”\n\nThis follows way made easy to show that easy Way leads to death as appointed.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to see death and burial as the end, like the deniers who wish death was the end and would that the return would be a total loss. Death and burial are the planting, like the humble fluid placed, not the end.\n\nFor the living who were told this is a reminder so let whoever wills be mindful, causes to die and be buried should make you take the Right Way while you will, before the soul reaches the collar bone and no healer saves, before woe and more woe, so dying and being buried become the sowing for the Gardens held in honour where dense orchards await, not the raging Flame that rips scalps where they remain for endless ages.",
  "22": "\"Then when He wills, He will resurrect them.\" promises resurrection after death and burial. The raising is by divine will.\n\n“Then when He wills, He will resurrect them” translates “thumma idha sha’a ansharah,” when He wills, He will resurrect, scatter, bring forth, as with “He will return you to it, and then simply bring you forth again,” “All will then be informed of what they have sent forth and left behind,” despite excuses the soul testifies against itself, the self-reproaching soul, the presentation where no secret stays hidden, the Trumpet blown, earth and mountains lifted and crushed, sky torn, Throne borne, and the Day every person will see what hands did and the disbeliever wishes to be dust. When He wills indicates the appointed time that never delays when it comes and can never be delayed, the Day fifty thousand years they see as far but We see as near, and the Day the Inevitable has come to pass.\n\nResurrect, or “ansharah,” is to spread, to publish, as with dense orchards, the Gardens where you will be amid cool shade and springs, and the spread scriptures wished for as personal letters.\n\nThis follows causes to die and be buried to show that burial is not the end, contrary to “Do they think they will be left without purpose? Were they not once a sperm-drop?” Is such a Creator unable?\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think resurrection is automatic without recompense, like honor expels inferior. Resurrection is for reckoning, as with every soul detained for what it has done, except the people of the right, and the fitting reward as generous gift.\n\nFor the condemned ungrateful, this verse should humble. The One who created you from a sperm-drop, ordained, eased the Way, caused to die and be buried, can resurrect when He wills, so you are not left without purpose, but are test subjects who will be informed, whose Gardens held in honour or Fire hold depends on grateful vs ungrateful, on whether you were mindful of the pages held in honour by angel-scribes honourable and virtuous, or among the disbelieving humans condemned ungrateful.",
  "23": "\"But no! They have failed to comply with what He ordered.\" is a rebuke after the human journey from sperm-drop, way easy, death, burial, resurrection when He wills. The failure is noted.\n\n“But no! They have failed to comply with what He ordered” translates “kalla lamma yaqdi ma amarah,” No! Not yet has he accomplished what He commanded, as with “But no! Indeed, they already know what We created them from” humbling, “But no! By the moon, Hell is mightiest catastrophe” after sceptics’ “What does Allah mean by this example,” and the despicable withholder, transgressor. Failed to comply indicates that despite being created from a drop, ordained, way eased, and told this revelation is truly a reminder on pages held in honour, they have not done what was ordered, like the denier who neither believed nor prayed, denied and turned away, swaggered, received woe, and the wicked’s four causes—not praying, not feeding poor, indulging, denying until inevitable came.\n\nWhat He ordered, or “ma amarah,” includes worship Allah alone, fear Him, obey the warner, seek forgiveness, show the Way grateful, fulfil vows, fear sweeping horror, give food despite desire for Allah alone, remember the Name morning and evening, prostrate part of night, glorify long, make room, give rightful share, guard chastity, keep trusts, and be properly observant, whose Gardens are held in honour.\n\nBut no, or “kalla,” denies that they have complied.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear failure as final, as with luxury deniers left for a little while with shackles. Failure here is call to comply now, before the Day they will come forth from graves swiftly, eyes downcast.\n\nFor the living who are told to let whoever wills be mindful of it, failure should become compliance. Comply with what He ordered now, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, seek forgiveness, so the Day all are informed of what they sent forth, your informing is “All this is surely a reward, your striving appreciated,” not “They have failed to comply.”",
  "24": "\"Let people then consider their food:\" invites reflection after the rebuke that they have failed to comply. Consideration starts with food.\n\n“Let people then consider their food:” translates “fa-li-yanzur al-insanu ila ta‘amihi,” let man look at his food, as with “Let the human look again at flawless heavens without finding flaw” and the Pen. Food here is the same provision as grain and plants and dense orchards produced by pouring rain, pastures brought forth, fresh water, and the Garden’s any fruit they desire, full cups, and the choking food in store for luxury deniers, and the oozing pus that only evildoers eat. Consider, or “yanzur,” is to look with insight, not just sight, as with the attentive ear that grasps the Ark.\n\nThis follows they have failed to comply to show that even compliance can be learned through food, and precedes “how We pour down rain in abundance and meticulously split the earth open for sprouts, causing grain to grow...,” detailing production.\n\nLet people consider their food is the same as “Have you considered the water you drink? Is it you who bring it down from clouds?” and “Have you considered what you sow? Is it you who cause it to grow?”\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit consideration to nutrition, like the despicable withholder’s ancient fables because blessed with wealth. Considering food is to see the Lord who smoothed earth like a bed, pegged mountains, created pairs, sleep, night, day, heavens, lamp, rain, and appointed Day, all for sustenance for you and your animals.\n\nFor the one who was nothing, then a drop, then Way eased, then death and burial, then resurrection when He wills, but who has failed to comply, considering food is the path to compliance, to be grateful, fulfil vows, give food despite desire, fear the sweeping horror, so considering food becomes the Gardens held in honour where considering is replaced by eating and drinking happily for what you used to do, where food is dense orchards, not the garden reduced to ashes.",
  "25": "\"how We pour down rain in abundance\" begins the detailed consideration of food, the first step. The rain is poured.\n\n“how We pour down rain in abundance” translates “anna sababna al-ma’a sabba,” We poured water pouring, abundantly, as with abundant rain showered after seeking forgiveness, spring flowing at will, flowing water if yours sinks, pouring water from rainclouds, grain and plants, dense orchards, and the provision from sources never imagined. Pouring in abundance, or “sabba,” is to gush, like the spring Salsabil and the crystal silver filled precisely, but here for earth.\n\nWe pour indicates the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between, the Most Compassionate, no one will dare speak to Him, the Blessed in Whose Hands rests all authority, Most Capable, who raised sky high and formed flawlessly, dimmed night, brought forth daylight, spread earth, brought forth water and pastures, set mountains, all as sustenance.\n\nThis follows let people consider their food to show how food begins, not from the despicable withholder, but from Us pouring.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to credit rain to the powerless force that would help instead of the Most Compassionate.\n\nFor practice, consider rain pouring and be grateful, show Way, choose grateful, give rightful share to the asker and deprived, let the poor enter your garden today, say “Allah willing,” so pouring becomes your Gardens under rivers for ever and ever where pouring is excellent provision granted to those who believe and do good, and where dense orchards await, not the dunes and shackles.\n\nAdditional: How We pour down rain in abundance also begins the detailed consideration of food, the first step, how He pours down rain in abundance, as with abundant rain showered after seeking forgiveness, spring flowing at will, flowing water if yours sinks who else could bring it, and the provision from sources never imagined. Pouring in abundance, or “sabba,” is to gush, like the spring Salsabil and the crystal silver filled precisely, but here for earth. We pour indicates the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between, the Most Compassionate, no one will dare speak to Him, the Blessed in Whose Hands rests all authority, Most Capable, who raised sky high and formed flawlessly, dimmed night, brought forth daylight, spread earth, brought forth water and pastures, set mountains, all as sustenance. We pour is mercy that makes the humble fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, and the earth smoothed like a bed, now produce, so pour becomes your Gardens’ springs and rivers under which rivers flow for ever and ever, where pouring is excellent provision granted to those who believe and do good.",
  "26": "\"and meticulously split the earth open ˹for sprouts˺,\" gives the second step after pouring rain in abundance. The earth is split for growth.\n\n“and meticulously split the earth open for sprouts” translates “thumma shaqaqna al-arda shaqqa,” then We split the earth with a splitting, as with the splitting of the sky into many gates on the appointed Day, but here splitting is for sprouts, mercy, not for terror. Splitting the earth open, or “shaqqa,” is to fissure, like the Pen’s precise splitting and the well-preserved Record, and the garden’s earth split for sprouts, the earth smoothed like a bed now split to bring forth.\n\nMeticulously indicates thorough, skillful splitting, not random, as with perfectly ordained, How excellent are We, and the precise crystals filled precisely.\n\nThis follows pouring rain to show that rain plus splitting produces.\n\nSplitting the earth open contrasts with the earth swallowing as it quakes violently for those who feel secure, and the earth and mountains lifted and crushed, sky torn.\n\nFor the living who failed to comply with what He ordered, splitting should make you comply, being mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, spending, and being saved from selfishness, so splitting becomes your grain and plants, grapes and greens, olives, palm trees, dense orchards, and the Gardens held in honour where splitting is the easy fruit made very easy to reach, not the Fire’s shackles.\n\nMore: Meticulously split the earth open for sprouts also gives the second step after pouring rain in abundance, the earth is split for growth, meticulously split, or “shaqaqna al-arda shaqqa,” then We split the earth with a splitting, as with the splitting of the sky into many gates on the appointed Day, but here splitting is for sprouts, mercy, not for terror. Splitting the earth open, or “shaqqa,” is to fissure, like the Pen’s precise splitting and the well-preserved Record, and the garden’s earth split for sprouts, the earth smoothed like a bed now split to bring forth. Meticulously indicates thorough, skillful splitting, not random, as with perfectly ordained, How excellent are We, and the precise crystals filled precisely. For the living who failed to comply with what He ordered, splitting should make you comply, being mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, spending, and being saved from selfishness, so splitting becomes your grain and plants, grapes and greens, olives, palm trees, dense orchards, and the Gardens held in honour where splitting is the easy fruit made very easy to reach, not the Fire’s shackles.",
  "27": "\"causing grain to grow in it,\" states the first produce after splitting the earth for sprouts. Grain is staple.\n\n“causing grain to grow in it” translates “fa-ambatna fiha habba,” We made grow in it grain, seeds, staples, as with producing by it grain and various plants, dense orchards, the rain that delights planters, the sowing “is it you who cause it to grow or is it We?” and the provision for you and your animals, mata‘an lakum wa li-an‘amikum. Grain, or “habba,” is the seed that delights, then withers to chaff, but here sustained, as with the Gardens’ any fruit they desire, full cups, and the excellent provision for ever and ever.\n\nCausing grain to grow indicates the same One who placed humble fluid in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, and who pours rain and splits earth, causes.\n\nThis follows splitting to show result.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to attribute grain to the withholder who says ancient fables because blessed with wealth and children, and whose snout will be marked.\n\nFor the one who was nothing, drop, hearing and seeing, Way shown, grateful or ungrateful, grain growing should make you grateful, fulfilling vows, feeding despite desire, so grain becomes the Gardens’ grain that is told to eat and drink happily, rewarded as good-doers, where grain is not the choking food and painful punishment in store, but the bliss and vast kingdom, where your striving has been appreciated and the Pages held in honour are yours.\n\nFurther: Causing grain to grow in it also states the first produce after splitting the earth for sprouts, grain is staple, as with producing by it grain and various plants, dense orchards, the rain that delights planters, the sowing “is it you who cause it to grow or is it We?” and the provision for you and your animals, mata‘an lakum wa li-an‘amikum. Grain, or “habba,” is the seed that delights, then withers to chaff, but here sustained, as with the Gardens’ any fruit they desire, full cups, and the excellent provision for ever and ever, splendid homes, and the vast kingdom and bliss. Causing grain to grow indicates the same One who placed humble fluid in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, and who pours rain and splits earth, causes. For the one who was nothing, drop, hearing and seeing, Way shown, grateful or ungrateful, grain growing should make you grateful, fulfilling vows, feeding despite desire, so grain becomes the Gardens’ grain that is told to eat and drink happily, rewarded as good-doers, where grain is not the choking food and painful punishment in store, but the bliss and vast kingdom, where your striving has been appreciated and the Pages held in honour are yours.",
  "28": "\"as well as grapes and greens,\" adds variety after grain. The provision diversifies.\n\n“as well as grapes and greens” translates “wa ‘inaban wa qadban,” grapes and moist greens, herbs, as with Gardens and vineyards for the righteous who will have salvation, dense orchards, grain and plants, olives, palm trees. Grapes, or “‘inab,” is the vine fruit that delights, like the full-bosomed maidens’ gardens, the pure wine flavoured with camphor and ginger, and the spring Salsabil. Greens, or “qadb,” is fresh herbage for animals and humans, as with bringing forth its water and pastures all as sustenance for you and your animals.\n\nThis follows grain to show that food is not monoculture, but variety, like the Pen’s many writings and the reminder’s many signs.\n\nGrapes and greens indicate that the One who smooths earth like a bed and pegs mountains also provides delicate and hardy.\n\nThis follows producing grain to add.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to take grapes and greens as mere produce to hoard and gather, like the hoarder the Flame summons. Produce here is to consider and to share, as with giving food despite desire.\n\nFor the living who were told to make room, give rightful share, and walk upright, grapes and greens should move you to consider your food, be grateful, and comply with what He ordered, so grapes and greens become your Gardens, vineyards, where any fruit they desire is yours, and dense orchards remain, where indulging until they face their Day is replaced by eating and drinking happily for what you used to do.\n\nAdditional: As well as grapes and greens also adds variety after grain, grapes and greens, as with Gardens and vineyards for the righteous who will have salvation, dense orchards, grain and plants, olives, palm trees. Grapes, or “‘inab,” is the vine fruit that delights, like the full-bosomed maidens’ gardens, the pure wine flavoured with camphor and ginger, and the spring Salsabil. Greens, or “qadb,” is fresh herbage for animals and humans, as with bringing forth its water and pastures all as sustenance for you and your animals. Grapes and greens indicate that the One who smooths earth like a bed and pegs mountains also provides delicate and hardy. To avoid crediting rain alone, remember the Producer, be grateful, fulfil vows, give food despite desire, so grapes and greens become your Gardens, vineyards, where any fruit they desire is yours, and dense orchards remain, where indulging until they face their Day is replaced by eating and drinking happily for what you used to do.",
  "29": "\"and olives and palm trees,\" continues the list after grapes and greens, adding two iconic trees. The pair is classic.\n\n“and olives and palm trees” translates “wa zaytunan wa nakhla,” olives and date palms, as with the fig and the olive, the palm trees whose harvest was for all its fruit in early morning for garden owners, the locust and the sundered, and the grain, grapes, greens. Olives and palm trees are long-lived, blessed trees that provide oil and dates, sustenance that stores, like the excellent provision granted to those who believe and do good for ever and ever, and the provision from sources never imagined.\n\nOlives and palm trees follow grapes and greens to show that variety includes staple, vine, herbage, oil, and dates, covering needs.\n\nThis is part of the “Consider your food” invitation, how We pour rain, split earth, cause grain, grapes, greens, olives, palms, dense orchards, all as sustenance.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to reduce olives and palms to commerce, like merchandise fanfare better than what is with Allah, or to the garden owners’ harvest for all without “Allah willing.” Their commerce is worship if considered as from Allah and shared.\n\nFor the one who has failed to comply with what He ordered, olives and palms should bring compliance, as with the Helpers who loved emigrants, the sincere repentance that perfects light, and the night worship more impactful, so olives and palms become your Gardens held in honour where olives’ oil and palms’ dates are among the any fruit they desire, told to eat and drink happily, rewarded as good-doers, where dense orchards await, not the Fire’s fruitless smoke shade.\n\nMore: And olives and palm trees also continues the list after grapes and greens, adding two iconic trees, olives and palm trees, as with the fig and the olive, the palm trees whose harvest was for all its fruit in early morning for garden owners, the locust and the sundered, and the grain, grapes, greens. Olives and palm trees are long-lived, blessed trees that provide oil and dates, sustenance that stores, like the excellent provision granted to those who believe and do good for ever and ever, and the provision from sources never imagined. Olives and palm trees follow grapes and greens to show that variety includes staple, vine, herbage, oil, and dates, covering needs. Olives and palms, like dense orchards, are signs for the mindful, like the reminder that would humble a mountain, the Pen, and the Ark. For the one who has failed to comply with what He ordered, olives and palms should bring compliance, as with the Helpers who loved emigrants, the sincere repentance that perfects light, and the night worship more impactful, so olives and palms become your Gardens held in honour where olives’ oil and palms’ dates are among the any fruit they desire, told to eat and drink happily, rewarded as good-doers.",
  "30": "\"and dense orchards,\" completes the food list after olives and palm trees. The orchards are intertwined, thick.\n\n“and dense orchards” translates “wa hada’iq ghulba,” thick, entwined gardens, as with dense orchards produced by pouring water, Gardens, vineyards, dense orchards for the righteous who will have salvation, and the Gardens under rivers for ever and ever where excellent provision is granted. Dense, or “ghulba,” is thick, lofty, as with the Gardens held in honour, the elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach, and the vast kingdom and bliss, fine silk, silver bracelets, and pure drink.\n\nDense orchards follows olives and palm trees to show that food consideration ends in lushness, like the earth spread for spacious pathways, now covered with dense growth.\n\nThis follows the earlier dense orchards produced by pouring water for grain, showing that dense is the culmination of pouring, splitting, grain, grapes, greens, olives, palms.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to see dense orchards as merely the fleeting world’s dense, to be loved and make you neglect the weighty Day ahead, indulging and amusing until they face their Day. Dense orchards here are signs for the mindful, like the reminder that would humble a mountain, the Pen, and the Ark.\n\nFor the one who was nothing worth mentioning, then a drop for test, shown grateful or ungrateful, dense orchards should make you grateful, not condemned ungrateful, fulfilling vows, fearing sweeping horror, giving food despite desire for Allah alone, so dense orchards become your Gardens held in honour where dense is not dunes of shifting sand but the Garden’s shade right above and fruit easy, where you recline on canopied couches never seeing scorching heat, and the difficult Day far from easy becomes easy for the persevering.\n\nFurther: And dense orchards also completes the food list after olives and palm trees, the orchards are intertwined, thick, dense, as with dense orchards produced by pouring water, Gardens, vineyards, dense orchards for the righteous who will have salvation, and the Gardens under rivers for ever and ever where excellent provision is. Dense, or “ghulba,” is thick, lofty, as with the Gardens held in honour, the elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach, and the vast kingdom and bliss, fine silk, silver bracelets, and pure drink. Dense orchards follows olives and palm trees to show that food consideration ends in lushness, like the earth spread for spacious pathways, now covered with dense growth. Dense orchards are the earthly foretaste of the Gardens held in honour, shade right above, silver vessels, crystal, ginger, Salsabil, eternal youths, vast kingdom, fine silk, silver bracelets, pure drink. To see dense orchards as merely the fleeting world’s dense, to be loved and make you neglect the weighty Day ahead, indulging and amusing until they face their Day, is to miss the sign. For the one who was nothing worth mentioning, then a drop for test, shown grateful or ungrateful, dense orchards should make you grateful, fulfilling vows, fearing sweeping horror, giving food despite desire for Allah alone, so dense orchards become your Gardens held in honour where dense is not dunes of shifting sand but the Garden’s shade right above and fruit easy, where you recline on canopied couches never seeing scorching heat, and the difficult Day far from easy becomes easy for the persevering.",
  "31": "\"and fruit and fodder\" closes the long catalogue of blessings that began several verses ago with rain, the earth splitting open, grain, grapes, greens, olives, palm trees, and dense orchards. The list now ends with two final gifts. \"Fruit\" refers to the fresh, sweet produce people eat for delight—grapes, dates, figs, and everything similar. It is not eaten only to survive; it is eaten for joy and refreshment. This tells us something about the Giver: He does not merely keep His creatures alive, He also gladdens them.\n\n\"Fodder\" is food for animals—grass, herbage, and plants that cattle, sheep, camels, and other beasts feed on. The Arabic word used here is \"abb.\" Early commentators explained it in slightly different ways. A report from Ibn Abbas, the Prophet's cousin and a great scholar among the Companions, describes \"abb\" as the herbage of the earth that animals graze upon. Others understood it more widely as every plant the earth brings forth. Either way, the message is the same: Allah's provision is not for humans alone. The creatures people own, ride, milk, and depend upon are also fed from the same earth, by the same Lord.\n\nNotice how complete the picture is. The surah began this whole section with the instruction, \"Let people then consider their food\" and then traced the journey step by step: rain poured down, the earth split open, grain grew, vines and trees followed, and now fruit for people and fodder for animals. Nothing in the chain was left to human power. People did not make the rain fall, nor did they design the seed. Elsewhere the Quran makes the same point with a gentle question: \"so that they may eat from its fruit, which they had no hand in making. Will they not then give thanks?\" The question is worth sitting with. Every meal is an invitation to gratitude, and every harvest is a quiet sign that the One who provides has not left us to guess about His care.",
  "32": "\"all as ˹a means of˺ sustenance for you and your animals\" gathers up everything listed before it—grain, grapes, greens, olives, palms, orchards, fruit, and fodder—and names its purpose in one short phrase. The Arabic word behind \"sustenance\" here is \"mata',\" and it carries a meaning the English word alone does not fully carry. \"Mata'\" means something given to be enjoyed for a while: a provision, a convenience, a loan that lasts for a time. Ibn Kathir points this out in his commentary, noting that these blessings are an enjoyment until an appointed term. They are real, delicious, and generous—but they are temporary.\n\nThat single idea quietly prepares the next verse. The surah has been describing food, drink, and comfort, and suddenly it will say, \"Then, when the Deafening Blast comes to pass\" The transition only makes sense because the blessings were always on loan. When we treat provision as a permanent possession, we forget the appointment at its end. When we treat it as \"mata',\" everything we enjoy carries a reminder written into it.\n\nThe phrase \"for you and your animals\" adds a second lesson in humility. Humans and cattle appear side by side in the same sentence, fed from the same rain and the same soil. A person may own fields and herds, yet he eats by the same giving that feeds his camel. Elsewhere the Quran describes rain bringing forth gardens and grain \"˹as˺ a provision for ˹Our˺ servants. And with this ˹rain˺ We revive a lifeless land. Similar is the emergence ˹from the graves˺.\" Look closely at that last sentence: the food we eat is itself an argument for resurrection. The Lord who revives dead earth every spring is the Lord who will bring people out of their graves. The meal on the table, received with thanks, becomes evidence for the meeting with its Giver.",
  "33": "\"Then, when the Deafening Blast comes to pass\" is the moment the whole surah has been moving toward. After the gentle scenes of rain, fields, and food, the tone changes with a single word. The Arabic term for this event is 'as-Sakhkhah', a word built from a root that speaks of a deafening cry, a thunderclap, a shout that overwhelms the ears. It is one of the Quran's names for the second blast of the Trumpet, the horn-blast at which the dead rise. It is called \"Deafening\" because it silences and stuns everything else; no voice, alarm, or noise in creation compares with it.\n\nThe Quran describes that moment elsewhere in vivid detail: \"The Trumpet will be blown and all those in the heavens and all those on the earth will fall dead, except those Allah wills ˹to spare˺. Then it will be blown again and they will rise up at once, looking on ˹in anticipation˺.\" Another passage says it will catch people in the middle of their ordinary lives: \"They must be awaiting a single Blast, which will seize them while they are ˹entrenched˺ in ˹worldly˺ disputes.\" The picture is sobering. Arguments in the marketplace, plans for the harvest, debts and dreams—all of it cut short by one sound.\n\nNotice also the small word translated \"when\" combined with \"comes to pass\" The verse does not say \"if.\" In Arabic the phrasing carries the weight of a settled fact, something already certain, only awaiting its hour. And the \"Then\" links the Blast directly to the food verses before it. People were enjoying sustenance, a loan of enjoyment for an appointed time, and the appointed end of that time arrives without warning. The lesson is not to hate this world's blessings—they are God's gifts—but to never let the feast make us forget the appointment that follows it.",
  "34": "\"on that Day every person will flee from their own siblings\" paints the first of several shocking pictures of the Hour. The Arabic verb behind \"flee\" describes running away in panic, hurrying to escape. What makes the image startling is who is being fled from. In the Arabia of the Prophet's time, your siblings and your tribe were your safety net. A person's brothers and sisters were his protectors, his defenders, his pride, and his backup in every crisis. If disaster struck, you ran toward your kin; you did not run from them.\n\nSo when the Quran says people will flee from their own siblings, it is announcing the total collapse of every support system humans build. On that Day, the bonds people leaned on become bonds people fear. Another passage describes the same scene with painful clarity: \"And no close friend will ask ˹about˺ their friends\" and the wicked will wish to ransom themselves from the punishment of that Day \"by their children\" and by \"their spouses, their siblings\". Why would anyone run from a brother or sister who loves them? The commentaries explain several layers of meaning. Each person will be drowning in his own concern, unable to carry anyone else's burden. Some will fear that their loved ones might ask them for help they cannot give. Others will fear that the very presence of family will remind them of duties they neglected—the children they did not guide, the kin they wronged.\n\nThere is also a quiet justice in the order of the verse. Many people build their whole identity on family name, tribe, and bloodline. They imagine that belonging to a respected line will count for something at the end. The verse gently strips that illusion away. On the Day every record is opened, a person's family cannot stand in for them, and the first people they distance themselves from are the very ones they once relied upon completely.",
  "35": "\"and ˹even˺ their mother and father\" deepens the shock of the previous verse. After siblings, the surah mentions parents—the two people whose love for a child usually asks for nothing in return. A mother carries her child for months, endures pain to bring it into the world, and spends years feeding, washing, worrying, and sacrificing sleep. A father works and protects, and often denies himself comforts so his children can have them. If any human love could shield a person on the Day of Judgment, parental love would be the strongest candidate.\n\nYet the verse says people will flee even from them. This is the Quran's way of telling us exactly how absolute the Day is. Every account is personal. No one's love, however deep, can transfer a single good deed into another person's record, and no one's sacrifice can carry another's sins. The Quran warns about this Day repeatedly so that it never slips from our minds: \"O humanity! Be mindful of your Lord, and beware of a Day when no parent will be of any benefit to their child, nor will a child be of any benefit to their parent.\"\n\nA simple comparison may help. Imagine a courtroom where the accused is a person's own child, and the parent is helpless to answer a single question on their behalf, helpless even to stand beside them. The love is still there, but the power to help is gone. That is the reality being described—but on a cosmic scale, before the Judge who knows everything.\n\nThere is comfort hidden in this warning, too. If no one else can save me, then no one else can block me, either. On that Day, family connections will not rescue the guilty—but they will also not prevent the humble person with no connections, no name, and no wealth from receiving complete justice. What will matter is what each soul personally brought: faith, honesty, repentance, and deeds done sincerely for Allah.",
  "36": "\"and ˹even˺ their spouse and children\" completes the list of the closest human attachments. The surah has moved through siblings, then parents, and now the two relationships that fill a person's daily emotional life. A spouse is the companion of decades—the one who knows your fears, shares your home, and stands beside you through illness and age. Children are the delight of the eyes, the ones a parent protects instinctively, often more fiercely than the parent protects himself.\n\nTo say a person will flee from exactly these people describes a fear so heavy it overwhelms the deepest instincts of the human heart. No one runs from their own child in a normal state of mind. But that Day is not normal. It strips every soul down to its own account. The Quran describes the wicked wishing to ransom themselves from the punishment of that Day \"by their children\"—and the same passage goes on: \"their spouses, their siblings\", their \"clan that sheltered them\", and \"everyone on earth altogether, just to save themselves\". Read that carefully: they would give away everyone they ever loved, all at once, to escape the Fire. The attachment they lived for becomes a price they would gladly pay.\n\nThe Quran also gives this truth a practical warning for life before that Day. It says: \"O believers! Indeed, some of your spouses and children are enemies to you, so beware of them.\" That does not mean family is bad; it means love of family can pull a person away from obedience—making him compromise his honesty, delay his repentance, or stay silent about wrong out of fear of displeasing them. The believers' response is balance: honor and cherish family, but never let any human being outrank Allah in one's loyalty.\n\nFor reflection: the healthiest way to love your family is to bring them with you toward Allah, not merely to depend on them at the end. Spouses and children can be a comfort in this world and a light in the next—for those who loved them within the limits of faith, not in place of it.",
  "37": "\"For then everyone will have enough concern of their own\" explains the strange fleeing described in the previous three verses. The word translated \"concern\" comes from the Arabic root that paints a person fully occupied, absorbed, and preoccupied—someone so loaded with his own matter that he has no attention left for anyone else. On that Day, this is not rudeness or betrayal of love; it is total incapacity. Every soul will be standing before its own record, and the weight of it will fill the person's entire horizon.\n\nThe Quran gives us glimpses of how heavy that Day will feel. In Surah Al-Hajj it says: \"The Day you see it, every nursing mother will abandon what she is nursing, and every pregnant woman will deliver her burden ˹prematurely˺. And you will see people ˹as if they were˺ drunk, though they will not be drunk; but the torment of Allah is ˹terribly˺ severe.\" A mother forgetting the baby at her breast—this is the strongest image of preoccupation the human mind can receive. If a nursing mother's attention can be torn from her infant, then no bond on earth can hold two people together on that Day.\n\nAn everyday picture may make the idea concrete. Think of a student sitting for the exam that decides his entire future, with minutes left and questions unanswered. His best friend beside him is also drowning. Neither can solve the other's paper. Love exists, but each hand has its own pen. Judgment Day is that reality in its final form: \"˹on that Day˺ each soul will know what ˹deeds˺ it has brought along\", as the next surah puts it.\n\nThis should shape how we live now. Since every soul will meet its own record alone, the wise person does not postpone faith waiting for family tradition, a spouse's encouragement, or society's approval. He settles his own account with Allah—through repentance, prayer, and honest deeds—while there is still time to do so.",
  "38": "\"On that Day ˹some˺ faces will be bright\" turns from the terror of the Blast to its result for two different groups of people. It is striking that the Quran again and again describes the Day of Judgment by faces. Faces are the most personal, most exposed part of a person—the part that cannot hide feelings. On that Day, what is hidden inside every heart will come out and settle on every face.\n\nThe Arabic word for \"bright\" suggests a radiance, a glowing freshness. Some early commentators compared it to the shining of the full moon. This brightness is not physical beauty; it is honor made visible. The Quran says elsewhere, \"On that Day ˹some˺ faces will be bright,\" and then adds the greatest reward of all in the very next words: \"looking at their Lord.\" That final privilege—seeing Allah—is the joy from which the face's radiance flows.\n\nThere is a beautiful teaching of the Prophet Muhammad, recorded in Bukhari and Muslim, that even small acts of faith will surface as light on that Day. He said that his followers will be brought forward on the Day of Resurrection with bright faces and limbs, shining from the traces of ablution—the ordinary washing believers perform before prayer five times a day. A routine act, done faithfully and quietly for years, becomes permanent radiance.\n\nIt is also worth noticing how this verse completes the story the surah began telling. Surah 'Abasa opened with a face: \"He frowned and turned ˹his attention˺ away,\" when a poor blind believer came seeking the Quran while the Prophet was speaking to a wealthy leader. Now, at the surah's end, faces appear again—some bright, some dark. The poor man who was frowned upon will be among the bright faces; worldly status will decide nothing. The surah that opened with a frown closes with radiance, and the whole story of honor is turned right side up.",
  "39": "\"laughing and rejoicing\" describes the bright faces of the previous verse in their full state. Two words are used, and together they carry a complete picture: laughter of ease and a heart delighting in good news. The laughter of the believers on that Day is not the laughter of mocking others, and it is not nervous relief that barely survives. It is the laughter of people whose fear has ended completely, whose books are settled, and whose home is secure forever.\n\nThe Quran deliberately contrasts this laughter with the laughter of this world. The wicked used to laugh at the believers first. Surah Al-Mutaffifin recalls their behavior: they \"used to laugh at the believers,\" wink at one another when the believers passed by, and call them astray. Then comes the reversal: \"But on that Day the believers will be laughing at the disbelievers,\" while the believers sit \"as they sit on ˹canopied˺ couches, looking on.\" The exchange of positions is exact. Those who were ridiculed for truth end up honored, and those who mocked from seats of power end up watching what they earned.\n\nAnother verse captures the reason for this joy: \"So Allah will deliver them from the horror of that Day, and grant them radiance and joy\". The joy is a gift, not a personal achievement. Believers will know that what saved them was Allah's mercy flowing from their faith and sincere deeds—not their cleverness, strength, or lineage.\n\nIn this world, believers are sometimes made to feel that faith is embarrassing—too simple, too restrictive, too unglamorous. They may be the subject of jokes for praying, dressing modestly, or refusing what everyone else approves. This verse and its companions are a promise of endurance: hold your peace, keep your faith, and let the mockery pass. Laughter at truth is temporary; the rejoicing of the saved is eternal. What a person laughs at today, and what he weeps over today, both reveal which face he is preparing for that Day.",
  "40": "\"while ˹other˺ faces will be dusty\" shifts to the second group, and the contrast could not be starker. In the previous verses there was radiance and laughter; now there is dust. In the language of the Quran's first audience, dust on the face was a sign of utter humiliation. A person humbled in battle or disgrace fell face-down in the dust; dignity meant keeping the face clean and head high. So a dusty face on Judgment Day is a picture of honor being stripped away in public.\n\nThe physical image also carries a spiritual one. These are the people who spent their lives chasing dust—wealth, applause, power—and now dust is all that settles on them. What they collected has abandoned them, and what they mocked has overtaken them. The Quran uses a similar contrast in Surah Al-Imran: \"On that Day some faces will be bright while others gloomy.\" And to the gloomy-faced, that passage continues, it will be said, \"Did you disbelieve after having believed? So taste the punishment for your disbelief.\" That verse adds an important detail: among the gloomy-faced will be some who once believed and then turned away. The warning is therefore not only for those who never believed, but for anyone who lets faith wither after it has grown.\n\nIt helps to remember that these face-verses follow the story this surah began. The surah opened with a believer—a poor, blind man—being treated as unimportant, while a man of standing received full attention. Now the standings are reversed by the Judge who sees hearts. Dust settles on the faces that looked down on others; brightness covers the faces that were once overlooked.\n\nThere is still time, and that is the mercy inside this warning. Everyone alive today still stands before that Day, not after it. The face that will be dusty or bright tomorrow is being prepared by choices made today—by humility or arrogance, faith or denial, compassion or contempt.",
  "41": "\"cast in gloom\" adds darkness to the dust of the previous verse. The Arabic word here is \"qatarah,\" and early scholars explained it as a blackness, like the soot and dark grime that settles over something. Picture a face layered: first dust, then a covering of darkness closing over it. Light has not just left the face; it has been replaced by its opposite.\n\nThis layered image matters. In the Quran, light is linked with guidance, faith, and Allah's pleasure, while darkness is linked with being lost. One passage describes the condition of those who refuse faith like someone in the depths of a dark sea, buried under wave after wave and cloud after cloud—\"Darkness upon darkness!\"—until the verse concludes: \"And whoever Allah does not bless with light will have no light!\" The gloom on these faces is the outward print of the inner state—a life that refused guidance ends in shadow.\n\nGrief also darkens a face. A person overwhelmed by terrible news is said to have his face fall or turn dark with anguish; the Quran uses that very description elsewhere about those receiving devastating news. So the gloom combines disgrace and despair: public humiliation plus the certainty that regret can no longer repair anything.\n\nNotice what is happening to the faces in these verses. In this world, people manage their faces—we compose our expressions, hide guilt, and present confidence. On that Day, no one manages anything. The face simply reports the truth of its owner. Reports from the early generations add that wasted opportunity deepens the darkness: how dark the face of a person who learned the truth and walked away from it.\n\nThe verse stands as an invitation in disguise. Darkness is announced now so that people can avoid it now. The door of repentance is open while a person breathes, and the Quran repeatedly promises that Allah forgives every sin of those who turn back to Him sincerely.",
  "42": "\"those are the disbelievers, the ˹wicked˺ sinners\" is the verdict that identifies the dusty, gloomy faces. After describing the condition, the Quran names the people. Two titles are used, and each one teaches something.\n\nThe word \"disbelievers\" translates the Arabic term 'kafir'. Its root meaning is to cover or conceal—like a farmer covering seeds with soil. A \"kafir\" is, at his core, one who covers the truth: he encounters the message of his Creator, senses its seriousness, and buries it under distractions, pride, or desires. Disbelief is not usually an intellectual accident; it is a stance of refusal, a hand pushed against guidance.\n\nThe second title, \"the ˹wicked˺ sinners\", translates 'fujjar', the plural of 'fajir'. Its root picture is of something bursting out of its proper place, like water breaking through a dam. A \"fajir\" is a person who bursts the limits Allah set—one who lives as if there are no boundaries. The pairing of the two words is deliberate and precise: what a person believes shapes how he behaves. When the heart covers the truth, the life bursts the bounds. Corruption on the outside is the fruit of denial on the inside.\n\nNow the surah's whole story stands complete. It opened with a moment when a sincere, blind seeker of the Quran was overlooked in favor of a self-sufficient leader, and heaven itself intervened to correct that moment. Then the surah walked through human creation from a sperm-drop, through death, burial, and resurrection, through rain and food, to the Blast and the faces of the Last Day. At the finish line, the two groups are named. The once-frowned-upon believer belongs to the bright, laughing faces; the honored denier belongs to the dark ones.\n\nThe lasting instruction is about sight. Humans judge by wealth, position, and appearance; Allah judges by faith, sincerity, and deeds. Whoever grasps that will never again measure a human being by his bank account—and will never again measure himself that way, either."
};
